5. Application Entry Point (API Controller)

app/application.rb
Sinatra controller remains thin. Its only job is request validation, calling the domain layer, and formatting responses.

ruby
Копировать
# frozen_string_literal: true

require 'sinatra/base'
require 'json'
require_relative './infrastructure/cats_unlimited_adapter'
require_relative './infrastructure/happy_cats_adapter'
require_relative './domain/dealer'

class CatsDealerApp < Sinatra::Base
  configure do
    set :show_exceptions, false
    disable :protection # For simplicity in example
  end

  before do
    content_type 'application/json'
  end

  get '/api/v1/best_cat' do
    location = params[:location]
    halt 400, { error: 'Location parameter is required' }.to_json if location.nil? || location.strip.empty?

    adapters = [CatsUnlimitedAdapter.new, HappyCatsAdapter.new]
    dealer = Dealer.new(adapters)
    
    result = dealer.find_best_deal(customer_location: location)

    if result
      status 200
      result.to_h.merge(message: "Best deal found!").to_json
    else
      status 404
      { message: 'No cats available at the moment from integrated shops.' }.to_json
    end
  end

  # Global error handler
  error ApiError do
    status 502
    env['sinatra.error'].message
  end
end
6. Test Coverage Strategy

Tests should cover three layers:

Unit Tests for Adapters: Use webmock to stub successful responses (JSON/XML), as well as malformed bodies and timeouts. Verify that they return an array of valid Cat structs or raise correct custom errors.
Unit Tests for Dealer: Do not hit the network here. Pass doubles/mocks of adapters directly into Dealer, forcing them to return pre-defined arrays of Cat objects. Check edge cases: empty list, one failing adapter, two identical prices.
Integration Test for Endpoint: Spin up the Sinatra app (Rack::Test), make a real GET request to /api/v1/best_cat, and assert the final combined JSON payload and HTTP status codes (200, 400, 404, 502).
